<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghat is an oasis town in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, near the Algerian border. It was a key stop on the trans-Saharan caravan route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> and a centre of the Kel Ajjer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> garrisoned it from 1875, and it later came under Italian rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ghat is the capital of the Ghat District in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, just east of the Algerian border. It lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and has a hot desert climate, with summer highs above 40 °C and about 8 millimetres of rain a year, which makes it one of the driest places on Earth. Prehistoric rock paintings and engravings survive in the neighbouring Tadrart Acacus and Tassili N'Ajjer mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In historical times Ghat was an important terminal point of a trans-Saharan trade route and a major administrative centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>. It was a stronghold of the Kel Ajjer Tuareg federation, whose territory covered most of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, including Ubari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabha-libya">Sabha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghadames">Ghadames</a>, and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. From the 5th century BCE to the 5th century CE the Fezzan was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a>, which ran the trade routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and the Sahelian states.</p><p>Portions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> were part of the Kanem Empire in the 13th and 14th centuries, and the Ottoman rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> asserted control there in the 17th century. The English traveller James Richardson described Ghat and its people in detail on his journeys of 1845 and 1846.</p><p>From 1911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> occupied Ghat and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>. Berber and Arab followers of the Sanusiya, a militant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order, resisted early Italian attempts at conquest, and Italian control remained precarious until at least 1923. To defend their position the Italians built the Fortress of Ghat on the hill of Koukemen. France occupied Ghat in 1943 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. On 21 November 1949 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> resolved that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> should become independent before 1 January 1952, and Fezzan Province was then absorbed into the Kingdom of Libya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Fortress of Ghat still dominates the town from Koukemen hill and is its main attraction. Ghat is a destination for visitors to the rock art and desert landscapes of the nearby mountains, and has its own airport.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ghat, Libya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The king of the Garamantes controlled trade routes that reached deep into sub-Saharan Africa, to the regions between the Niger Bend and Lake Chad; the southern oases of <b>Ghat</b> and Barkat were bases for traders travelling into tropical Africa.</blockquote><a href="/culture/garamantes">Garamantes · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kel Ajjer emerged as a distinct confederation of the eastern Tuareg, occupying the Tassili n'Ajjer plateau and the oases of <b>Ghat</b> and Djanet in present-day south-eastern Algeria and south-western Libya.</blockquote><a href="/state/kel-ajjer">Kel Ajjer</a></li></ul></section>
